      "id": 1,
      "text": "Alcorcón’s name comes from the Arabic word for hill, reflecting its origins on a knoll.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ayto-alcorcon.es/en/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In medieval times a watchtower stood on Alcorcón’s hill, watching Christian troops and protecting nearby Arab cities.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.ayto-alcorcon.es/en/history"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The football club AD Alcorcón, founded in 1971, hosts matches at the 5,100‑seat Municipal de Santo Domingo stadium.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AD_Alcorc%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 2009 the club stunned Real Madrid with a 4‑0 victory in the Copa del Rey first‑leg match at home.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/AD_Alcorc%C3%B3n"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Alcorcón’s historic landmarks include the Iglesia de Santa María la Blanca and the town hall, both central to its heritage.",
